D2 = {
"pw-01-hydrogen-production-clean-energy": [
 ("How an electrolyser makes hydrogen","Understand",9,"An electrolyser uses electric current to split water into hydrogen and oxygen. Knowing how much power and water go in tells you how much gas should come out.",
  ["Water in, direct current across a stack of cells, hydrogen out at one side and oxygen at the other.","As a rule of thumb, making 1 kg of hydrogen takes about 9 litres of pure water and roughly 50 to 55 kWh of electricity.","The water must be very clean, so a treatment step removes salts before the stack.","Heat is a by-product; cooling water carries it away and the temperature has a safe window."],
  "A stack draws 2,000 kW for 10 hours = 20,000 kWh. At 52 kWh per kg, output = 20,000 / 52 = about 385 kg of hydrogen, using about 385 x 9 = 3,465 litres of pure water. At AED 0.25 per kWh the power cost is AED 5,000, or about AED 13 per kg.",
  "Calculate hydrogen output, water use and power cost per kg for one 8-hour run, using sample figures.","Show units on every line, check the arithmetic twice, and state water and power each per kg."),
 ("Gas safety, pressure and hazardous areas","Apply",10,"Hydrogen is light, leaks easily and burns with a flame that is hard to see in daylight. Safety rests on rated equipment, ventilation and strict permits.",
  ["Hazardous areas are marked on a drawing; only equipment rated for the zone may be used there.","Fixed gas detectors raise an alarm at a low share of the lower flammable limit (about 4% hydrogen in air); know the alarm levels and the action at each.","Pressure vessels have relief valves set above working pressure; never isolate one without a signed permit.","Static electricity can ignite gas, so use earthed tools and wear the rated antistatic clothing."],
  "A detector reads 10% of the lower flammable limit near a compressor. The operator stops the compressor, keeps people upwind, tells the control room at 09:42 and waits for a gas test below 5% of the lower flammable limit before any restart.",
  "Mark the hazardous zones on a sample plant sketch and write the first three actions for a gas alarm.","Match the zones to the drawing, order alarm actions safely, and check that no step relies on bypassing a protection."),
 ("Purity, output and efficiency results","Evaluate",9,"A plant earns its keep by delivering gas in specification and using little energy. Daily records show whether it is doing that.",
  ["Purity is checked on a sample at set times; a batch below the grade is held and not dispatched.","Efficiency is the energy in the hydrogen divided by the electricity used; falling efficiency can signal a worn stack.","Track kg produced, kWh used and hours running each day in one table.","Compare each day with target and write the likely cause of every miss."],
  "Day 1: 4,800 kg made on 252,000 kWh = 52.5 kWh per kg. Day 2: 4,300 kg on 241,000 kWh = 56.0 kWh per kg. Target is 54 kWh per kg, so Day 2 is 2 kWh per kg over; the log notes a cell cleaning delayed by 3 hours.",
  "Fill a five-day table from sample data, compute kWh per kg and flag the days above target.","Give every figure a unit, and flag days over target with a cause or next check."),
],
"pw-02-green-hydrogen-export-corridors": [
 ("Choosing a carrier and a route","Analyse",9,"Hydrogen is hard to ship as a gas, so it often travels as ammonia or in another carrier. The choice changes cost, safety and what the buyer must do on arrival.",
  ["Compare carriers on how much hydrogen they hold, how they are stored and what conversion steps are needed at each end.","Ammonia holds about 17.6% hydrogen by weight, so 1 tonne of ammonia carries about 176 kg of hydrogen.","List the legs of a route: production site, port, voyage, receiving terminal, final user.","Every extra conversion step loses energy, so count the steps."],
  "A buyer needs 880 tonnes of hydrogen. At 0.176 tonnes of hydrogen per tonne of ammonia, the ship must carry 880 / 0.176 = 5,000 tonnes of ammonia, and to allow for about 5% lost on the way it must load 5,000 / 0.95 = about 5,263 tonnes.",
  "Compare two carriers for one route on four points and recommend one with reasons.","Give units with the numbers, count at least one loss or conversion step, and explain the recommendation."),
 ("Dangerous goods, leaks and paperwork","Apply",10,"Some carriers are toxic or flammable. Rules on labelling, handling and emergency response must be met before anything leaves the port.",
  ["Each carrier has a safety data sheet; read the hazards, safe handling and first-aid sections before any job.","Dangerous goods documents must match the cargo exactly; a mismatch stops loading.","Know the exclusion zone and wind direction rules for transfer hoses.","Practice a leak drill on paper: alarm, upwind move, shut transfer, report."],
  "During a transfer, a hose coupling drips at 14:05. The loader stops the pump, closes the shore valve, moves the team 100 metres upwind to the muster point and reports to the terminal control room within 5 minutes.",
  "Write a one-page checklist for a loading day covering documents, equipment checks and the leak drill.","Assign an owner to every check, put drill steps in a safe order, and match paperwork items to the cargo."),
 ("Certificates, contracts and delivery results","Evaluate",9,"Buyers pay for proof that the hydrogen is green and that the amount arrived. Certificates and contract terms decide who carries the risk.",
  ["A guarantee of origin links each tonne to how the energy was produced; one certificate must not be counted twice.","In a take-or-pay contract the buyer pays for a minimum volume even if it takes less.","Track tonnes planned, tonnes delivered and tonnes with valid certificates.","Late or missing certificates should be flagged before the ship sails, not after."],
  "Planned 3,200 tonnes, delivered 2,900 tonnes, of which 2,750 tonnes carry valid certificates. Certified share = 2,750 / 3,200 = 86% of plan; 150 tonnes were delivered without a valid certificate and are logged for follow-up.",
  "Use sample data for three shipments to compute delivered and certified shares and list the gaps.","Calculate percentages with units shown, and give every gap an action and an owner."),
],
"pw-03-renewable-energy-integration": [
 ("Matching supply to demand","Apply",9,"Sun and wind do not follow the clock. Integration means forecasting what they will give, comparing it with demand and filling the gap.",
  ["Capacity factor is the energy a plant actually makes divided by what it would make running at full power all the time; solar is often 20% to 30%, wind varies by site.","Energy in MWh equals power in MW times hours.","Plot hourly supply and demand on one chart and mark the hours with surplus and shortfall.","Storage or backup covers shortfalls; surplus charges storage or is curtailed."],
  "A 50 MW solar plant with a 25% capacity factor makes 50 x 24 x 0.25 = 300 MWh per day. If the site uses 400 MWh per day, renewables meet 300 / 400 = 75% of demand and 100 MWh must come from storage or the grid.",
  "Find the hours of surplus and shortfall and the daily share met by renewables, using sample hourly data.","Show units on every figure, mark surplus and shortfall hours, and state the share met as a percent."),
 ("Electrical safety and grid connection rules","Apply",10,"Connecting a power source to a grid can harm people and equipment if it is done wrongly. Rules and licensed people protect both.",
  ["Lockout and tag-out: isolate, lock, test dead, then work; never trust a switch position alone.","Work at height needs a harness, a rescue plan and a second person on the ground.","The grid code sets limits on voltage, frequency and how a plant behaves in a fault; compliance is tested before connection.","Solar arrays stay live in daylight, so treat them as live even when the grid is off."],
  "Before cleaning a string of panels, the technician isolates the inverter, tests the cables at 0 V with a rated tester, fits a lock and tag, and notes the time 08:20 on the permit. The lock stays until the same person removes it.",
  "Write the lockout steps for an inverter job and the checks before a grid connection test.","List the steps in order, include the test-dead step, and name a licensed person for electrical work."),
 ("Measuring performance and curtailment","Evaluate",9,"Reports show how much clean power was used, wasted or missed. Good decisions come from the numbers, not from guesses.",
  ["Track energy produced, used on site, stored and curtailed each month in MWh.","Compare actual output with the expected output for the sunshine or wind measured.","Curtailment (clean power switched off and wasted) is a loss; find whether the cause is grid limits, faults or poor forecasts.","Report in a table a manager can read in one minute."],
  "Month: produced 7,800 MWh, curtailed 390 MWh = 5% curtailed. Site demand 9,000 MWh; renewables used 7,410 MWh = 82% of demand. Target 85%, so the gap of 3 percentage points links to the curtailed 390 MWh.",
  "Fill a monthly table for sample data and recommend one change to cut curtailment.","Calculate percentages correctly, name the cause of loss, and link the recommendation to a number."),
],
"pw-04-smart-manufacturing-automation": [
 ("Sensors, PLCs and the data trail","Understand",8,"A smart line senses what is happening, decides with a controller and records the result. Knowing the chain helps you trace a fault.",
  ["A sensor measures something such as position, temperature or count and sends a signal.","A PLC (programmable logic controller) reads inputs, runs a stored program in a repeating scan and switches outputs such as motors and valves.","Data is time-stamped and stored so trends can be studied later.","Draw the chain from sensor to PLC to actuator to screen for one machine."],
  "A bottle filler counts 120 bottles per minute. The sensor sends 2 pulses per bottle, so the PLC sees 240 pulses per minute. If it reads only 200 pulses per minute, either the line slowed or a sensor is missing pulses, so check the sensor first.",
  "Draw the sensor-to-screen chain for one machine and list what would be logged each minute.","Name every stage, label signal types and give log fields units."),
 ("Machine guarding and lockout","Apply",10,"Automated machines can start without warning. Guards and lockout are the main protection.",
  ["Never remove or bypass a guard, light curtain or emergency stop.","Lockout every energy source: electrical, air, hydraulic, stored spring or gravity.","Test that the machine will not start after locking, then work.","Robot zones have marked limits; enter only with the zone switched to a safe mode."],
  "To clear a jam on a packing machine, the operator presses stop, isolates power and air, fits a personal lock, releases trapped air pressure of 6 bar, presses start to prove nothing moves, then clears the jam.",
  "Write a lockout sequence for one machine listing every energy source and the proving step.","List all energy sources, include the proving step and bypass no guard."),
 ("OEE and deciding what to improve","Evaluate",9,"Overall equipment effectiveness shows where time and output are lost, so effort goes where it pays.",
  ["OEE equals availability times performance times quality.","Availability is run time divided by planned time.","Performance is actual speed divided by ideal speed.","Quality is good units divided by total units."],
  "Planned 480 minutes, run 408 minutes: availability 85%. Ideal 100 units per minute, actual 90: performance 90%. 36,720 made, 35,620 good: quality 97%. OEE = 0.85 x 0.90 x 0.97 = 74%. Biggest loss is availability, so look at stops first.",
  "Compute OEE for a sample shift and rank the three losses.","Show each factor as a percent, calculate the product correctly and name the biggest loss with a suggested check."),
],
"pw-05-digital-twin-simulation": [
 ("What a digital twin is and what it needs","Understand",8,"A digital twin is a model fed by real measurements so it follows the real asset. It is only useful if its inputs and limits are clear.",
  ["List what the twin represents, such as a pump, a building or a plant section, and what it leaves out.","Name the data feeds: sensor readings, maintenance records and drawings.","Set how often data updates, for example every 1 minute or every 1 hour.","Write the questions the twin is meant to answer before building it."],
  "A pump twin uses 4 sensors reading once per minute: 4 x 60 x 24 = 5,760 readings per day. At 10 bytes per reading that is about 57,600 bytes, small enough to store for years.",
  "Write a one-page scope for a twin of one asset: purpose, data feeds, update rate and what is left out.","State the purpose as a question, give every feed a unit and rate, and list exclusions."),
 ("Data quality, drift and protecting data","Apply",9,"Bad data gives confident wrong answers. Protecting the asset data matters as much as modelling it.",
  ["Sensor drift is slow error; compare readings with a reference at set intervals.","Check for gaps, frozen values and impossible numbers before using data.","Share only the data agreed; remove names and locations that are not needed.","Keep a log of every data change and who made it."],
  "A temperature sensor reads 61.5 degrees C while a reference gauge reads 60.0 degrees C. Drift = 1.5 degrees C, 2.5% of the reading. It is above the 1.0 degrees C limit, so the sensor is flagged for recalibration and its data is marked.",
  "Run a data check on a sample file and list issues, fixes and what you would not share.","Count issues, record fixes and state sharing limits."),
 ("Validating the model and using it to decide","Evaluate",10,"A model must be compared with real measurements before it guides a decision. Validation says how far it can be trusted.",
  ["Compare model output with measured values over the same period.","Calculate error as the difference divided by the measured value.","Set an acceptable error before testing, not after.","Use the model to test a change only inside the range it was validated for."],
  "Measured outlet flow 120 m3/h, model 126 m3/h. Error = (126 - 120) / 120 = 5%. Limit is 3%, so the model is retuned and tested again before it is used to judge a new valve setting.",
  "Validate a sample model on five data points, compute errors and decide pass or retune.","Calculate errors with units, set the limit first and match the decision to the result."),
],
}
